## Purpose

Before designing or implementing relay reachability changes, reconcile this security work with the concurrent database refactor after it lands.

## Execution hold

Do not claim this item until Max confirms the database refactor has landed and is stable. Its backlog ID was not identifiable on 2026-07-21; add that ID as an explicit dependency when known.

## Work

- Rebase on the post-refactor branch and identify the canonical relay persistence interfaces and backend migration mechanism.
- Map registration leases, inbox routing, identity keys, room discovery, rate state, and any new database ownership boundaries affected by private reachability/contact grants.
- Record compatibility constraints for in-memory and SQLite backends, deployed relay data, rolling upgrades, rollback, and production/dev modes.
- Update B259 and downstream tasks if the refactor changes their assumptions; do not implement the security protocol in this task.

## Acceptance evidence

- A short checked-in design input identifies exact post-refactor modules/schema surfaces and migration constraints.
- Downstream tasks reference the actual interfaces rather than pre-refactor guesses.
- The database-refactor backlog dependency is wired once its ID is known.